WEBVTT

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.564
Every morning, around ten, Margaret makes tea.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.978
The kettle. The toaster. Lunch in the microwave.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.248
Every home has a rhythm. And the power meter already knows it.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.607
Forty-two percent of Canadians aged eighty-five and over live alone.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:37.044
In a study of people over ninety, eight times out of ten, when they fell alone and couldn't get up, they didn't use their alarm.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.235
A pendant only works if you wear it. And press it.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.982
Kettle needs nothing from her.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.236
No cameras. No wearables. No buttons. Just the kettle.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:01:01.094
A small plug learns her mornings. The raw power readings never leave her home. Only moments, like "kettle on."

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.870
But one morning, the kettle stays quiet.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.599
So Kettle asks Margaret first. A call, and a soft light in her kitchen.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.673
No answer.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.187
So it doesn't call a call centre. It asks someone close.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.556
Priya lives two minutes away.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.553
And then… the kettle.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.645
Everyone can breathe.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:48.657
It works both ways, too. One tap, and a warm light glows in her kitchen.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.022
And it's built for Alberta winters.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:04.388
January thirteenth, twenty twenty-four. Minus thirty. The grid at its limit. At six forty-four, every phone in Alberta buzzed.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.439
Kettle makes that alert say one more thing. Check on Margaret.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:25.814
We replayed more than nine thousand real days, from twenty real homes, through the same code. It recognises the kettle almost every time. And every check-in starts with a call to her.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:34.620
Japan has trusted a kettle with this since two thousand and one. Every Calgary home already has a meter.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.000
No cameras. No wearables. Just someone who notices.

